---
/*
 * The landing page.
 *
 * ORDER IS AN ARGUMENT, and this one runs: here is the promise (hero) -> prove it by letting
 * me do it (demo) -> here is everything there is to learn (verbs) -> here is how I get in
 * (start) -> here is what it costs and how you actually get in today (invite). Each section
 * is eyebrow -> headline -> one-sentence lede -> the proof, so the rhythm repeats without the
 * layout repeating.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ONE SENTENCE CARRIES THE WHOLE PAGE: "A SIGNAL ANNOUNCES. IT NEVER LOCKS."
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * It is stated four times, in the same words, and never paraphrased into something weaker:
 *   Hero .... the lede, in bold, as the second half of the promise
 *   Verbs ... "Every one of them announces. None of them lock." — the same claim, narrowed
 *             to the five commands
 *   Demo .... the outcome copy, where the reader presses the button on a file someone else
 *             is already on and watches it go through. "Nothing stopped you."
 *   (and the demo's screen-reader verdict, which says it in the same words)
 *
 * WHY THIS SENTENCE AND NOT ANOTHER. It is the one thing about this product a reader is most
 * likely to get wrong, because every neighbouring tool in the category is a lock, and getting
 * it wrong turns the whole thing back into the friction story the operator retired. It is
 * also literally true rather than a positioning choice — Hero.astro's header carries the
 * source trace, and the word "claim" was removed from this whole directory in the same pass
 * because it quietly smuggles the old framing back in a friendlier word.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE WAS RE-JUDGED AND KEPT. "Your agents know what each other are doing." was
 * tested against the bar it has to clear — is it a promise about the reader's own agents, or
 * a description of a service? — and it is the former. See Hero.astro. Do not churn it.
 *
 * WHAT WAS CUT, and why, because "less is more" is only real if something goes:
 *   - the separate "why CommonSwarm" essay section. The demo argues it better, in one click.
 *   - a features grid. Five commands is not a feature grid, it is a vocabulary — and the
 *     eleven flag chips that had grown onto those five cards WERE one, so they went too,
 *     along with the footnote whose only job was to apologise for them (Verbs.astro).
 *   - the "Or set the project once" env-variable card (Start.astro). It taught a workaround
 *     for a problem the reader no longer has, now that `accept` saves the project.
 *   - the six-row facts table AND the email form in the closing card (Invite.astro). A spec
 *     sheet and a button that cannot act, in the last screenful, where the page should be
 *     asking for one thing. The close points at /download and /start instead.
 *   - the ethos line as a hero pull-quote. "Friction is justified only by irreversibility"
 *     is a builder's rule; in the largest type on the page it makes friction the subject,
 *     which is precisely the framing the operator retired. It stays in the spec.
 *   - the install one-liner as the hero CTA. The installer is not published and the host is
 *     undecided, so it would be a placeholder in the most prominent position on the site.
 *     It lives in step one of "how it works", with its caveat attached to it.
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT HAVE, and each absence is a decision, not an
 * oversight: no pricing table, no feature grid, no testimonials, no logo wall, no comparison
 * table, no FAQ, no metrics. We have no customers yet and inventing any of it is the one
 * defect this repo treats as more serious than a bug.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * HEADING PUNCTUATION — ONE RULE, WHOLE SITE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * DISPLAY AND SECTION HEADINGS END IN A FULL STOP. That is `.h-display` (the hero h1 and
 * /download's h1), every `.h-section` h2, and the three `.st-title` step headings on /start.
 * The voice is declarative — "Your agents know what each other are doing." — and a full stop
 * is what makes it a statement rather than a label.
 *
 * NOTHING ELSE TAKES ONE: eyebrows, Card titles, tab labels, form labels, the `.wh__t` and
 * `.ai__t` step titles inside a section, and any heading whose text is data (the /app
 * workspace name). Those are labels for a thing, not sentences about it.
 *
 * This was applied at roughly 60% before — "Find out who's already there." next to "What you
 * run once you're invited" — which reads as inattention rather than as a system. If a new
 * heading is added, it takes the rule or the rule is wrong; it does not take an exception.
 *
 * Header and footer nav (SiteHeader/SiteFooter) point at /#how-it-works. That id exists here
 * — the section is #how-it-works and its first step is #install. Renaming either breaks a
 * link in a file this page does not own.
 *
 * INTEGRATION: this page used to override Base's footer slot with landing/SiteFoot.astro.
 * The site had three footers; it now has one, in SiteFooter.astro, which every page reaches
 * by passing nothing. SiteFoot also asserted the repository is "private during dogfood",
 * which is no longer true — it answers 200 logged out.
 */

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Hero from "../components/landing/Hero.astro";
import Demo from "../components/landing/Demo.astro";
import Verbs from "../components/landing/Verbs.astro";
import Start from "../components/landing/Start.astro";
import Invite from "../components/landing/Invite.astro";

const title = "CommonSwarm — your agents know what each other are doing";
/* Carries the page's one framing sentence, because this is the copy a stranger reads before
 * they ever reach the page — in a search result, in a link preview, in a shared message. */
const description =
  "Each agent says what it is picking up before it starts, and the rest read it and route around it. A signal announces; it never locks. Five commands, driven from the CLI.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/">
  {/* is:inline and in <head>, so the attribute is set before the body is parsed. Setting it
      from a bundled module further down the document would paint the sections VISIBLE and
      then hide them, which is a flash rather than an entrance. */}
  <script is:inline slot="head">
    (() => {
      // motion.css's contract for the JS reveal path: set [data-motion-js] on <html>,
      // observe .js-reveal, add .is-in on entry. Nothing is hidden unless this runs, so a
      // browser without IntersectionObserver simply gets the page with everything visible.
      if (!("IntersectionObserver" in window)) return;
      if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) return;

      const root = document.documentElement;
      root.setAttribute("data-motion-js", "");

      // Failsafe. That attribute is the thing that hides the sections, so if anything below
      // throws — or simply never runs — the page would hold content permanently invisible.
      // This is the one guarantee that must not depend on the rest of the script, so it is
      // armed immediately and only stood down once the observer is actually watching.
      let wired = false;
      setTimeout(() => {
        if (!wired) root.removeAttribute("data-motion-js");
      }, 2000);

      const reveal = () => {
        const io = new IntersectionObserver(
          (entries) => {
            for (const entry of entries) {
              if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
              entry.target.classList.add("is-in");
              io.unobserve(entry.target);
            }
          },
          // Fires a little before the element's top edge reaches the fold, so the motion has
          // finished by the time the reader's eye arrives.
          { rootMargin: "0px 0px -6% 0px", threshold: 0.06 }
        );
        for (const el of document.querySelectorAll(".js-reveal")) io.observe(el);
        wired = true;
      };

      if (document.readyState === "loading") {
        document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", reveal, { once: true });
      } else {
        reveal();
      }
    })();
  </script>

  <Hero />
  <Demo />
  <Verbs />
  <Start />
  <Invite />
</Base>
